Make each scan stop at the next opener. Use `([^*
]+?)` for single `*`, `([^_
]+?)` for `_`, and `\[([^\[\]
]*)\]` for links. Each measured about 1 ms at 32 KB. For the heading, match greedily with `^ {0,3}(#{1,6})(?:[ 	]+(.*))?$` (0 ms at 32 KB) and strip the closing `#` run in Dart code (trimRight, then drop trailing `#`s only when a space or tab precedes them). A trailing regex like `[ 	]+#+[ 	]*$` is quadratic again. Add one test that times a long adversarial line.

Optional, given 'viable not flawless'. Allow one level of parentheses in the URL: `\(([^()
]*(?:\([^()
]*\)[^()
]*)*)\)`. Strip code spans last, or protect their contents first.

findings.evidence:
`_heading.firstMatch(text.trim())` uses `^...[^
]*?...$` without multiLine, so it matches only when the whole text is a single heading line. For any other text, `_withoutMarkers` strips the `#` markers and the line keeps body style. 35_markdown-text's body is `'# Heading 1

This is **bold** text ...'`.

votes.reasoning:
The claim holds when you read the code. `_heading` (text.dart:71) is `^ {0,3}(#{1,6})[ 	]+([^
]*?)[ 	#]*$` with no multiLine flag, so `^` and `$` anchor to the whole trimmed string, and `[^
]*?` cannot cross a newline. Any Text with more than one line, such as 35_markdown-text's `# Heading 1

This is **bold** ...`, therefore fails the heading match, `style` stays null, and `_withoutMarkers` strips the `#` with its multiLine regex. The heading line then renders in body style. The reviewer's probe output agrees: 35_markdown-text_375.txt shows one TEXT node at 14.0/w400 whose content begins `Heading 1

This is bold text...`, the same style as the paragraph after it.

This is not a violation. basic_catalog_implementation_guide.md line 27 says that when no Markdown renderer is available, the renderer should "fallback to rendering the raw text" and "ideally attempt to strip common Markdown markers", and the fallback does exactly that. The behaviour also matches its own docs: the doc comment at text.dart:30-32 ("a `body` text that is one Markdown heading line"), README.md:211-212 and CHANGELOG.md:31 ("a one-line Markdown heading"). Real Markdown rendering goes through the `A2uiMarkdown` hook (lines 52-54), which an app can supply. The result is a cosmetic gap in an optional, documented fallback and nothing a maintainer would block on. The proposed per-line `Text.rich` fix is small and uses stock widgets, so it is a reasonable optional polish. Leaving the code as documented is also acceptable.

